# SCOPED ON PURPOSE — one path, nothing else.
#
# A repo-wide `* text=auto` here would renormalise every tracked file in a
# single commit. core.autocrlf=true on the machines this is built on, and a
# whole-file CRLF<->LF rewrite has twice turned a 50-line change into a
# 3,000-line diff and made ownership of the real change unreadable. So this
# file exists for exactly one reason and takes exactly one line.
#
# gradle-wrapper.jar is a binary that is now tracked (see the negation in
# .gitignore). git detects it as binary today, but that detection is
# heuristic; a line-ending conversion applied to a zip corrupts it, and a
# corrupt wrapper is worse than the missing one it replaced. State it.
gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar binary

# Byte-exact on every checkout, Windows included. index.html loads these two
# with a sha384 `integrity` hash. With core.autocrlf=true a Windows checkout
# rewrote their line endings, the hash stopped matching, the browser refused
# both files and every page rendered blank (2026-10-08). `-text` means git
# never converts them. They are LF in the index today, so this changes no
# bytes in the repo, only what a checkout is allowed to do to them.
src/main/resources/static/js/vendor/react.production.min.js -text
src/main/resources/static/js/vendor/react-dom.production.min.js -text

# Shell scripts run under sh/bash, which reads a CRLF shebang as a missing
# "/bin/sh\r" interpreter. The Dockerfile copies gradlew into a Linux image, so
# a CRLF copy from a Windows checkout breaks the image build. Already LF here.
gradlew eol=lf
*.sh eol=lf
